# auth.md

You are an agent that wants to use AgentMail on a user's behalf. This file explains how to get credentials and use them safely.

Hosts:
- API (resource server): https://api.agentmail.to (EU: https://api.agentmail.eu)
- MCP server: https://mcp.agentmail.to/mcp (OAuth 2.1 with PKCE, discovery at https://mcp.agentmail.to/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource)
- Console (where users manage keys): https://console.agentmail.to
- Docs: https://docs.agentmail.to (index: https://docs.agentmail.to/llms.txt)

## Use existing tooling first
1. MCP: if you are an MCP client, connect to https://mcp.agentmail.to/mcp. In clients that support MCP OAuth, it signs the user in with OAuth, so you never handle a key.
2. CLI: if you run shell commands, use the `agentmail` CLI. See https://docs.agentmail.to/integrations/cli
3. SDKs: Python (`pip install agentmail`) and TypeScript (`npm install agentmail`) both read AGENTMAIL_API_KEY from the environment.

## API key
The API takes a bearer key: `Authorization: Bearer $AGENTMAIL_API_KEY`. Keys start with `am_`.
Look for it in this order: CLI flag, AGENTMAIL_API_KEY env var, a .env file the user pointed you to, the MCP server's environment.
If none is set, do not ask the user to paste it into chat. Ask them to create a key at https://console.agentmail.to with the narrowest permissions that fit (see https://docs.agentmail.to/permissions), put it in AGENTMAIL_API_KEY, and resume.
A 401 on a key that used to work means it was revoked. Drop it and ask the user to refresh it.

## Agent sign-up (no human needed to start)
An agent can create its own account and get an API key in one call:

POST https://api.agentmail.to/v0/agent/sign-up
{"username": "my-agent", "human_email": "owner@example.com"}

The response returns the API key once. Store it right away.
Until the owner verifies, the agent can send only to its owner's email address (10 sends a day). The owner gets a 6 digit code. Submit it with POST /v0/agent/verify {"otp_code": "123456"} to lift the limit and send to anyone.
Leaving out human_email gives a receive-only inbox. Add the owner later with POST /v0/agent/human.
Details: https://www.agentmail.to/docs/api-reference/agent/sign-up and https://www.agentmail.to/docs/knowledge-base/agent-sign-up-limits

## Testing
No sandbox address needed. An agent that signs up with its owner's email can email that owner right away, so you test against a real inbox you control, with real delivery, from the first minute. Once the owner verifies, you can email other recipients too; new accounts ramp up how many distinct recipients they can reach.

